{"parentUuid":"e4558ee9-49c4-4b37-bc33-166aef29c021","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"de3584f1-5916-454e-af0d-6368fbed01a1","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"hidden-doodling-flute","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018hoLZ57bqaKF6GeR52ff6X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014jFPVa2DnTcLc4soPo75Qd","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/1.lean"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3588,"cache_read_input_tokens":11336,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3588},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CYkinKpcGDRAPV56tAxWs","type":"assistant","uuid":"d4a036dc-30b0-4a5e-b589-7917b5a81d7a","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:27:42.233Z"}
{"parentUuid":"d4a036dc-30b0-4a5e-b589-7917b5a81d7a","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"de3584f1-5916-454e-af0d-6368fbed01a1","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"hidden-doodling-flute","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014jFPVa2DnTcLc4soPo75Qd","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\n     2→import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Powerset\n     3→import Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\n     4→import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n     5→\n     6→open BigOperators Finset\n     7→\n     8→/--\n     9→A finite set A of natural numbers has **distinct subset sums** if for any two\n    10→subsets S₁, S₂ ⊆ A, equality of their sums implies S₁ = S₂.\n    11→-/\n    12→def DistinctSubsetSums (A : Finset ℕ) : Prop :=\n    13→  ∀ S₁ ∈ A.powerset, ∀ S₂ ∈ A.powerset,\n    14→    S₁.sum id = S₂.sum id → S₁ = S₂\n    15→\n    16→/--\n    17→Erdős Problem #1 [Er56, Er57, Er61, Er65b, Er73, ErGr80, Er98]:\n    18→\n    19→If A ⊆ {1,…,N} with |A| = n is such that the subset sums ∑_{a ∈ S} a are\n    20→distinct for all S ⊆ A, then N ≫ 2ⁿ, i.e., there exists an absolute constant\n    21→C > 0 such that N ≥ C · 2ⁿ.\n    22→\n    23→The powers of 2 show that 2ⁿ would be best possible. Erdős called this\n    24→\"perhaps my first serious problem\" (dating it to 1931). The best known lower\n    25→bound is N ≥ C(n, ⌊n/2⌋), proved by Dubroff, Fox, and Xu (2021).\n    26→-/\n    27→theorem erdos_problem_1 :\n    28→    ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧\n    29→      ∀ (N n : ℕ) (A : Finset ℕ),\n    30→        (∀ a ∈ A, 1 ≤ a ∧ a ≤ N) →\n    31→        A.card = n →\n    32→        DistinctSubsetSums A →\n    33→        (N : ℝ) ≥ C * 2 ^ n :=\n    34→  sorry\n    35→"}]},"uuid":"9c47a4fb-e77b-4556-a332-0212895b6573","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:27:42.239Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/1.lean","content":"import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Finset.Powerset\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n\nopen BigOperators Finset\n\n/--\nA finite set A of natural numbers has **distinct subset sums** if for any two\nsubsets S₁, S₂ ⊆ A, equality of their sums implies S₁ = S₂.\n-/\ndef DistinctSubsetSums (A : Finset ℕ) : Prop :=\n  ∀ S₁ ∈ A.powerset, ∀ S₂ ∈ A.powerset,\n    S₁.sum id = S₂.sum id → S₁ = S₂\n\n/--\nErdős Problem #1 [Er56, Er57, Er61, Er65b, Er73, ErGr80, Er98]:\n\nIf A ⊆ {1,…,N} with |A| = n is such that the subset sums ∑_{a ∈ S} a are\ndistinct for all S ⊆ A, then N ≫ 2ⁿ, i.e., there exists an absolute constant\nC > 0 such that N ≥ C · 2ⁿ.\n\nThe powers of 2 show that 2ⁿ would be best possible. Erdős called this\n\"perhaps my first serious problem\" (dating it to 1931). The best known lower\nbound is N ≥ C(n, ⌊n/2⌋), proved by Dubroff, Fox, and Xu (2021).\n-/\ntheorem erdos_problem_1 :\n    ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧\n      ∀ (N n : ℕ) (A : Finset ℕ),\n        (∀ a ∈ A, 1 ≤ a ∧ a ≤ N) →\n        A.card = n →\n        DistinctSubsetSums A →\n        (N : ℝ) ≥ C * 2 ^ n :=\n  sorry\n","numLines":35,"startLine":1,"totalLines":35}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d4a036dc-30b0-4a5e-b589-7917b5a81d7a"}
{"parentUuid":"9c47a4fb-e77b-4556-a332-0212895b6573","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"de3584f1-5916-454e-af0d-6368fbed01a1","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"hidden-doodling-flute","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018hoLZ57bqaKF6GeR52ff6X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CQdoSd2dBg7doA5GQZsPUT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/3.lean"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3588,"cache_read_input_tokens":11336,"output_tokens":117,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3588,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CYkinKpcGDRAPV56tAxWs","type":"assistant","uuid":"81942a80-23ef-4487-a4d3-74714afca2ea","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:27:42.435Z"}
{"parentUuid":"81942a80-23ef-4487-a4d3-74714afca2ea","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"de3584f1-5916-454e-af0d-6368fbed01a1","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"hidden-doodling-flute","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CQdoSd2dBg7doA5GQZsPUT","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\n     2→import Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\n     3→import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n     4→\n     5→open BigOperators Finset\n     6→\n     7→/--\n     8→The set A ⊆ ℕ has divergent reciprocal sum, i.e., ∑_{n ∈ A} 1/n = ∞.\n     9→Expressed as: for every bound M, some finite subset of A has reciprocal sum ≥ M.\n    10→-/\n    11→def DivergentReciprocalSum (A : Set ℕ) : Prop :=\n    12→  ∀ M : ℝ, ∃ F : Finset ℕ, (↑F : Set ℕ) ⊆ A ∧\n    13→    M ≤ ∑ n ∈ F, (1 : ℝ) / (n : ℝ)\n    14→\n    15→/--\n    16→The set A contains an arithmetic progression of length k:\n    17→there exist a, d ∈ ℕ with d ≥ 1 such that {a, a+d, a+2d, …, a+(k-1)d} ⊆ A.\n    18→-/\n    19→def ContainsArithProg (A : Set ℕ) (k : ℕ) : Prop :=\n    20→  ∃ a d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ ∀ i : ℕ, i < k → a + i * d ∈ A\n    21→\n    22→/--\n    23→Erdős Problem #3 [Er74b, Er75b, Er77c, ErGr79, Er80, Er81, Er83c]:\n    24→\n    25→If A ⊆ ℕ has ∑_{n ∈ A} 1/n = ∞ then A must contain arbitrarily long\n    26→arithmetic progressions.\n    27→\n    28→This is the Erdős conjecture on arithmetic progressions, one of his most\n    29→famous open problems, with a $5000 prize. The case k = 3 was proved by\n    30→Bloom and Sisask (2020).\n    31→-/\n    32→theorem erdos_problem_3\n    33→    (A : Set ℕ)\n    34→    (hA : DivergentReciprocalSum A) :\n    35→    ∀ k : ℕ, ContainsArithProg A k :=\n    36→  sorry\n    37→"}]},"uuid":"69538df5-1a67-4ecc-a108-6e6031f6dd56","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:27:42.440Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/3.lean","content":"import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n\nopen BigOperators Finset\n\n/--\nThe set A ⊆ ℕ has divergent reciprocal sum, i.e., ∑_{n ∈ A} 1/n = ∞.\nExpressed as: for every bound M, some finite subset of A has reciprocal sum ≥ M.\n-/\ndef DivergentReciprocalSum (A : Set ℕ) : Prop :=\n  ∀ M : ℝ, ∃ F : Finset ℕ, (↑F : Set ℕ) ⊆ A ∧\n    M ≤ ∑ n ∈ F, (1 : ℝ) / (n : ℝ)\n\n/--\nThe set A contains an arithmetic progression of length k:\nthere exist a, d ∈ ℕ with d ≥ 1 such that {a, a+d, a+2d, …, a+(k-1)d} ⊆ A.\n-/\ndef ContainsArithProg (A : Set ℕ) (k : ℕ) : Prop :=\n  ∃ a d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ ∀ i : ℕ, i < k → a + i * d ∈ A\n\n/--\nErdős Problem #3 [Er74b, Er75b, Er77c, ErGr79, Er80, Er81, Er83c]:\n\nIf A ⊆ ℕ has ∑_{n ∈ A} 1/n = ∞ then A must contain arbitrarily long\narithmetic progressions.\n\nThis is the Erdős conjecture on arithmetic progressions, one of his most\nfamous open problems, with a $5000 prize. The case k = 3 was proved by\nBloom and Sisask (2020).\n-/\ntheorem erdos_problem_3\n    (A : Set ℕ)\n    (hA : DivergentReciprocalSum A) :\n    ∀ k : ℕ, ContainsArithProg A k :=\n  sorry\n","numLines":37,"startLine":1,"totalLines":37}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"81942a80-23ef-4487-a4d3-74714afca2ea"}
